---
/**
 * Site-wide search (T702, SRCH-01, SRCH-02).
 *
 * > **SRCH-01** THE site SHALL provide client-side search per locale over
 * > titles, symptoms, glossary terms and aliases, and part numbers.
 * > **SRCH-02** WHEN a visitor searches a regional alias (e.g. `balatas`),
 * > THE results SHALL include the canonical entry (GLO-03).
 *
 * ## One page, two URLs, one component (I18N-01)
 *
 * The same seam every other collection page in this repo shares: the segment
 * is translated (`/en/search/` and `/es/buscar/`), `getStaticPaths` reads
 * both values from `src/i18n/routes.ts`'s registry, and neither locale is a
 * redirect of the other.
 *
 * ## What is indexed, and why not everything
 *
 * Four collections today — `glossary`, `problems`, `parts`, `mods` — each
 * mapped to the collection-agnostic `SearchDocument` shape
 * `src/lib/search.ts` defines. Adding a fifth is one more `getCollection`
 * call and one more small `.map()` below; the matching logic in
 * `src/lib/search.ts` never changes (see that module's docstring).
 *
 * - **`procedures` is not indexed yet.** T502 has not given it a route to
 *   link a result to (`COLLECTION_ROUTE_SEGMENTS` has no `procedures` row),
 *   and a search result with nowhere to land is a worse answer than no
 *   result. The collection is empty today regardless; this is a forward
 *   note, not a workaround for a current failure.
 * - **`garage` is deliberately never indexed.** 002 MIG-03 makes a user's
 *   garage entries private data behind Supabase RLS. This page is public and
 *   statically built; indexing garage entries would publish private records
 *   in the built HTML of every reader's search results, not just the
 *   owner's. `community` is left out for the same "read the spec line, not
 *   the ambition" reason: SRCH-01 names titles, symptoms, glossary terms and
 *   aliases, and part numbers — not the community directory, which already
 *   has its own on-page search (T703a).
 *
 * ## Two filters, one shape — the glossary's rule, generalized
 *
 * `type` (a result kind pill) and `query` (free text) AND together and both
 * default to permissive, reproducing the full server-rendered listing on
 * load — `src/lib/search.ts`'s `matchesSearchFilter`, the same contract
 * `glossary-filter.ts` and `problems-filter.ts` state for their own pages.
 * Progressive enhancement follows the same pattern: the toolbar is laid out
 * only once `BaseLayout` marks the document `html.js`, so a visitor with no
 * JavaScript sees the complete, unfiltered result list and no dead control.
 *
 * **One difference from those two (PR #106 code review, SCF-06):**
 * `matchesSearchFilter` does not normalize the query text itself — the
 * `input` handler below calls `normalizeForSearch` exactly once per
 * keystroke and stores the result in `state.normalizedQuery`, so the
 * per-card loop that follows (221 cards at this index's current size) never
 * repeats that work. `glossary-filter.ts` and `problems-filter.ts` still
 * normalize inside their own `matchesFilter`, once per card per keystroke —
 * fine at their smaller scale, and not this task's collection to change.
 *
 * ## Haystack built from the rendered card (SCF-06)
 *
 * As with every other filtered listing on this site, the search index is
 * never serialized into the page a second time. The client script reads each
 * card's own already-rendered title, subtitle, snippet, badge chips, code
 * chips and "extra" chips (aliases, symptom bullets, cross-reference rows —
 * whichever the type carries) and builds the haystack from that
 * (`src/lib/search.ts`'s `buildSearchHaystack`), the same discipline
 * `[glossarySegment].astro` and `[problemsSegment].astro` already document at
 * length.
 *
 * **The snippet is the one exception, and it is deliberate (PR #106,
 * SCF-06):** `problems` entries' summaries average over a thousand
 * characters, and across 221 cards that was the index's single largest
 * weight contributor — measurably so on CI's Lighthouse budget, which
 * `/es/buscar/` (whose `prose.es` runs longer than `prose.en` throughout this
 * corpus) missed by more than `/en/search/` did for exactly that reason.
 * `splitSnippet` (`src/lib/search.ts`) divides a long snippet into a short
 * `visible` prefix and a `hidden` remainder rendered in a `display: none`
 * span — see that function's own docs for why this, and not a `title`
 * attribute carrying the whole snippet a second time, is the fix that
 * actually reduces page weight. `element.textContent` still walks into the
 * hidden span, so `buildSearchHaystack` sees the complete original snippet
 * and no word is lost to search, only to the card's own display.
 *
 * ## No vehicle-fitment filter here (deliberate scope cut)
 *
 * Every other listing page on this site layers FIT-03's dim-and-tag vehicle
 * filter on top of its own facets. This page does not: SRCH-01/SRCH-02 name
 * nothing about fitment, and wiring FIT-03 across four collections whose
 * `fitment` shapes are not being merged into one `SearchDocument` here would
 * be a second feature riding on this task's branch. A reader who wants
 * fitment-aware results follows a result to its own collection page, which
 * already carries the vehicle filter.
 *
 * refs specs/001-foundation (SRCH-01, SRCH-02, GLO-03, I18N-01, I18N-04, I18N-08)
 */
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import {
  LOCALES,
  LOCALE_BCP47,
  isLocale,
  localeHref,
  type Locale,
} from "../../i18n/routing";
import {
  COLLECTION_ROUTE_SEGMENTS,
  collectionRoutePath,
  collectionRouteParams,
  collectionRoutePaths,
  entryRoutePath,
} from "../../i18n/routes";
import {
  glossarySystemLabel,
  problemSeverityLabel,
  searchTypeLabel,
  t,
} from "../../i18n/ui";
import { formatCount, glossaryTermDomId } from "../../lib/glossary-filter";
import { problemRoutePath } from "../../lib/problems";
import {
  SEARCH_DOCUMENT_TYPES,
  splitSnippet,
  type SearchDocument,
  type SearchDocumentType,
} from "../../lib/search";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return collectionRouteParams("search", "searchSegment");
}

const { locale: param } = Astro.params;
if (!isLocale(param)) {
  throw new Error(`Unknown locale segment: ${String(param)}`);
}
const locale: Locale = param;
const strings = t(locale);
const routes = collectionRoutePaths("search");

const collator = new Intl.Collator(LOCALE_BCP47[locale], {
  sensitivity: "base",
});

const otherLocales = LOCALES.filter((candidate) => candidate !== locale);

/**
 * `doc.href` is unprefixed and already this locale's own route (the same
 * contract `entryRoutePath` and `collectionRoutePath` promise elsewhere on
 * this site) — `null` in, `null` out, so a document with no route to link to
 * renders its title unlinked rather than linking somewhere plausible-but-wrong
 * (`[partsSegment].astro`'s and `[modsSegment].astro`'s own rule).
 */
function docHref(routePath: string | null): string | null {
  return routePath === null ? null : localeHref(locale, routePath);
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * One adapter per indexed collection — the only collection-specific code on
 * this page. Each maps that collection's own already-typed entries into the
 * shared `SearchDocument` shape; `src/lib/search.ts`'s matching functions
 * never see which collection a document came from.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */

const glossaryEntries = await getCollection("glossary");
const glossaryDocuments: SearchDocument[] = glossaryEntries.map((entry) => {
  const { system, aliases } = entry.data;
  const gloss = otherLocales
    .map((other) => entry.data.prose[other].title)
    .filter((term) => term.length > 0);

  return {
    id: `glossary:${entry.id}`,
    type: "glossary",
    href: `${collectionRoutePath("glossary", locale)}#${glossaryTermDomId(entry.id)}`,
    title: entry.data.prose[locale].title,
    subtitle: gloss.length > 0 ? gloss.join(" / ") : null,
    snippet: entry.data.prose[locale].summary,
    badges: [glossarySystemLabel(strings, system)],
    codes: [],
    /**
     * SRCH-02: every alias renders on the canonical entry's own card, term
     * and countries together (`rin (CR/MX)`) — the same text
     * `[glossarySegment].astro`'s alias chips show, so a reader who searches
     * `balatas` finds this card exactly because it already says `balatas`.
     */
    extra: aliases.map(
      (alias) => `${alias.term} (${alias.countries.join("/")})`
    ),
  };
});

const problemsSegment = COLLECTION_ROUTE_SEGMENTS.problems[locale];
const problemEntries = await getCollection("problems");
const problemDocuments: SearchDocument[] = problemEntries.map((entry) => {
  const { data } = entry;
  const prose = data.prose[locale];

  return {
    id: `problems:${entry.id}`,
    type: "problems",
    href: problemRoutePath(problemsSegment, prose.slug),
    title: prose.title,
    subtitle: null,
    snippet: prose.summary,
    badges: [
      glossarySystemLabel(strings, data.system),
      problemSeverityLabel(strings, data.severity),
    ],
    codes: [],
    /** SRCH-01's "symptoms" — the same resolved text the problems index shows. */
    extra: data.symptoms.map((id) => prose.symptoms[id] ?? id),
  };
});

const partsEntries = await getCollection("parts");
const partsDocuments: SearchDocument[] = partsEntries.map((entry) => {
  const { id, oemNumber, system, crossReferences } = entry.data;
  const local = entry.data.prose[locale];
  const crossRefs = crossReferences ?? [];

  return {
    id: `parts:${id}`,
    type: "parts",
    href: entryRoutePath("parts", id, locale),
    title: local.title,
    subtitle: null,
    snippet: local.summary,
    badges: [glossarySystemLabel(strings, system)],
    /** SRCH-01's "part numbers": the OEM number and every cross-referenced one. */
    codes: [oemNumber, ...crossRefs.map((cross) => cross.partNumber)],
    extra: crossRefs.map((cross) => `${cross.brand} ${cross.partNumber}`),
  };
});

const modsEntries = await getCollection("mods");
const modsDocuments: SearchDocument[] = modsEntries.map((entry) => {
  const { id, system } = entry.data;
  const local = entry.data.prose[locale];

  return {
    id: `mods:${id}`,
    type: "mods",
    href: entryRoutePath("mods", id, locale),
    title: local.title,
    subtitle: null,
    snippet: local.summary,
    badges: [glossarySystemLabel(strings, system)],
    codes: [],
    extra: [],
  };
});

const documents = [
  ...glossaryDocuments,
  ...problemDocuments,
  ...partsDocuments,
  ...modsDocuments,
].sort((a, b) => collator.compare(a.title, b.title));

/** Only types with at least one document get a pill (the glossary page's rule). */
const typeOptions = (SEARCH_DOCUMENT_TYPES as readonly SearchDocumentType[])
  .filter((type) => documents.some((doc) => doc.type === type))
  .map((type) => ({ code: type, label: searchTypeLabel(strings, type) }));
---

<BaseLayout
  locale={locale}
  routePathByLocale={routes}
  title={`${strings.searchHeading} — ${strings.siteTagline}`}
  description={strings.searchIntro}
>
  <section class="search" data-search>
    <div class="search__intro">
      <h1>{strings.searchHeading}</h1>
      <p>{strings.searchIntro}</p>
    </div>

    {
      documents.length === 0 ? (
        <p class="search__empty">{strings.searchEmpty}</p>
      ) : (
        <>
          <div class="search__toolbar" data-search-toolbar>
            <div class="search__input">
              <label class="visually-hidden" for="search-input">
                {strings.searchInputLabel}
              </label>
              <svg
                class="search__input-icon"
                width="18"
                height="18"
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="2"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                aria-hidden="true"
                focusable="false"
              >
                <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="7" />
                <path d="m21 21-4.3-4.3" />
              </svg>
              <input
                id="search-input"
                type="search"
                autocomplete="off"
                data-search-input
                placeholder={strings.searchInputPlaceholder}
              />
            </div>
            {typeOptions.length > 0 && (
              <div
                class="search__types"
                role="group"
                aria-label={strings.searchFilterTypeLabel}
              >
                <button type="button" data-type="" aria-pressed="true">
                  {strings.searchFilterTypeAll}
                </button>
                {typeOptions.map((option) => (
                  <button
                    type="button"
                    data-type={option.code}
                    aria-pressed="false"
                  >
                    {option.label}
                  </button>
                ))}
              </div>
            )}
          </div>

          <p
            class="search__count"
            data-search-count
            data-count-template={strings.searchCountTemplate}
            data-total={String(documents.length)}
            aria-live="polite"
          >
            {formatCount(
              strings.searchCountTemplate,
              documents.length,
              documents.length
            )}
          </p>

          <ul class="search__list" data-search-list>
            {documents.map((doc) => {
              const href = docHref(doc.href);
              /**
               * SCF-06 follow-up (PR #106 review): `visible` and `hidden`
               * are complementary, non-overlapping halves of `doc.snippet`
               * (`splitSnippet`'s own docs walk through why — the first
               * version of this fix duplicated the whole snippet into a
               * `title` attribute, which made the page *larger*, not
               * smaller). `hidden` renders inside a `.visually-hidden` span
               * below, so `element.textContent` — what
               * `buildSearchHaystack` reads — sees the complete original
               * text, while only `visible` costs any layout or paint.
               */
              const snippet = splitSnippet(doc.snippet);
              return (
                <li class="search-result" data-type={doc.type}>
                  <span class="search-result__type">
                    {searchTypeLabel(strings, doc.type)}
                  </span>
                  <h2 class="search-result__title">
                    {href === null ? doc.title : <a href={href}>{doc.title}</a>}
                    {doc.subtitle !== null && (
                      <span class="search-result__subtitle">
                        {doc.subtitle}
                      </span>
                    )}
                  </h2>
                  <p class="search-result__snippet">
                    {snippet.visible}
                    {snippet.hidden !== "" && (
                      <span
                        class="search-result__snippet-rest"
                        aria-hidden="true"
                      >
                        {snippet.hidden}
                      </span>
                    )}
                  </p>
                  {(doc.badges.length > 0 || doc.codes.length > 0) && (
                    <p class="search-result__chips">
                      {doc.badges.map((badge) => (
                        <span class="chip search-result__badge">{badge}</span>
                      ))}
                      {doc.codes.map((code) => (
                        <span class="chip datum search-result__code">
                          {code}
                        </span>
                      ))}
                    </p>
                  )}
                  {doc.extra.length > 0 && (
                    <p class="search-result__extra">
                      {/*
                       * One chip holding every item, joined — not one chip
                       * per alias/symptom. Perf fix (PR #106 follow-up,
                       * SCF-06): a glossary term with several aliases or a
                       * problem with several symptom bullets used to add one
                       * `<span>` per item, and across 221 cards that was 655
                       * extra elements — measurably real page weight, not
                       * noise (see the commit message for the before/after
                       * byte counts). The joined string still carries every
                       * word a reader could search for (SRCH-02's alias
                       * recall is unchanged: `buildSearchHaystack` reads
                       * this element's `textContent`, which still contains
                       * every alias), it is simply one node instead of many.
                       */}
                      <span class="chip search-result__extra-item">
                        {doc.extra.join(" · ")}
                      </span>
                    </p>
                  )}
                </li>
              );
            })}
          </ul>

          <p class="search__none" data-search-none hidden>
            {strings.searchNoResults}
          </p>
        </>
      )
    }
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import {
    buildSearchHaystack,
    matchesSearchFilter,
    type SearchFilterState,
  } from "../../lib/search";
  import { formatCount } from "../../lib/glossary-filter";
  import { normalizeForSearch } from "../../lib/text";

  /**
   * Client-side filtering (T702). Enhancement only — the toolbar is laid out
   * only when the document is marked script-capable, so a visitor with no
   * JavaScript sees the complete listing and no dead control. Only DOM
   * wiring lives here: which elements to read, what to toggle. Every
   * decision (does this card match? what does the counter say? what counts
   * as its searchable text?) is a pure function in `src/lib/search.ts`.
   */
  function enhance(root: HTMLElement): void {
    const toolbar = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-search-toolbar]");
    const input = root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("[data-search-input]");
    const count = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-search-count]");
    const none = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-search-none]");
    if (!toolbar || !input || !count || !none) return;

    /**
     * Plain `textContent` — which is exactly why `.search-result__snippet`'s
     * `display: none` remainder span (`splitSnippet`'s `hidden` half, see the
     * server template's comment) works with no special-casing here:
     * `textContent` walks into hidden descendants regardless of their CSS
     * `display`, so a truncated card's full, untruncated snippet text is
     * already part of what this reads.
     */
    const textOf = (element: Element, selector: string): string[] =>
      [...element.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(selector)].map(
        (node) => node.textContent ?? ""
      );

    // The haystack is read from each card's own rendered text — never a
    // second server-serialized copy — and built exactly once here, not on
    // every keystroke (the glossary page's own rule, for the same reason).
    const cards = [...root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".search-result")].map(
      (element) => ({
        element,
        type: element.dataset.type ?? "",
        haystack: buildSearchHaystack({
          title: textOf(element, ".search-result__title"),
          subtitle: textOf(element, ".search-result__subtitle"),
          snippet: textOf(element, ".search-result__snippet"),
          badges: textOf(element, ".search-result__badge"),
          codes: textOf(element, ".search-result__code"),
          extra: textOf(element, ".search-result__extra-item"),
        }),
      })
    );

    const template = count.dataset.countTemplate ?? "";
    const total = Number(count.dataset.total ?? cards.length);
    const buttons = [
      ...toolbar.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-type]"),
    ];
    let state: SearchFilterState = { type: "", normalizedQuery: "" };

    function apply(): void {
      let shown = 0;
      for (const card of cards) {
        const visible = matchesSearchFilter(card, state);
        card.element.hidden = !visible;
        if (visible) shown += 1;
      }
      count!.textContent = formatCount(template, shown, total);
      none!.hidden = shown > 0;
    }

    for (const button of buttons) {
      button.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const type = button.dataset.type ?? "";
        state = { ...state, type };
        for (const other of buttons) {
          other.setAttribute(
            "aria-pressed",
            String((other.dataset.type ?? "") === type)
          );
        }
        apply();
      });
    }

    input.addEventListener("input", () => {
      // Normalized exactly once per keystroke, here — not inside
      // `matchesSearchFilter`, which would otherwise redo the same
      // NFD-decompose-and-strip-marks work once per rendered card (221 at
      // this index's current size) on every single keystroke (PR #106 code
      // review, SCF-06's Lighthouse performance budget).
      state = { ...state, normalizedQuery: normalizeForSearch(input.value) };
      apply();
    });

    apply();
  }

  for (const root of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-search]")) {
    enhance(root);
  }
</script>

<style is:global>
  /*
   * Palette from specs/001-foundation/design/HANDOFF-DESIGN.md, scoped to
   * this page via the `.search` prefix — the same substitute for Astro's
   * scoped CSS the glossary page's own docstring explains (a per-node
   * `data-astro-cid-…` attribute at this many result cards is real page
   * weight for no reader benefit).
   */
  .search {
    --paper: #f7f4ee;
    --panel: #ffffff;
    --ink: #2b2620;
    --muted: #6e6559;
    --muted-strong: #55493c;
    --line: #e4ddd1;
    --chip: #efe9dd;
    --rust-deep: #93441f;

    font-family: Archivo, "Helvetica Neue", Arial, sans-serif;
    color: var(--ink);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    max-width: 75rem;
  }

  .search .visually-hidden {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    margin: -1px;
    padding: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  .search .search__intro h1 {
    margin: 0 0 0.375rem;
    font-size: 1.875rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.3px;
  }

  .search .search__intro p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .search .search__input {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.625rem;
    flex: 1 1 20rem;
    background: var(--panel);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .search .search__input input {
    flex: 1;
    border: 0;
    background: transparent;
    font: inherit;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--ink);
    min-height: 1.75rem;
  }

  .search .search__input input::placeholder {
    color: var(--muted);
    opacity: 1;
  }

  .search .search__types {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .search .search__types button {
    font: inherit;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
    min-height: 2.75rem;
    background: var(--panel);
    color: var(--muted-strong);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .search .search__types button[aria-pressed="true"] {
    background: var(--ink);
    border-color: var(--ink);
    color: var(--paper);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .search .search__count,
  .search .search__none,
  .search .search__empty {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .search .search__list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .search .search-result {
    background: var(--panel);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .search .search-result[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  .search .search-result__type {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 1px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .search .search-result__title {
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .search .search-result__title a {
    color: var(--rust-deep);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .search .search-result__title a:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .search .search-result__subtitle {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .search .search-result__snippet {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--muted-strong);
  }

  /*
   * SCF-06 follow-up (PR #106 review): `splitSnippet`'s `hidden` half,
   * present only so `element.textContent` — what the client script's
   * `buildSearchHaystack` call reads — sees the complete original snippet
   * (SRCH-01 recall). Deliberately `display: none`, not this page's
   * `.visually-hidden` (the sr-only recipe used for the search box's own
   * `<label>`): that recipe keeps content in the accessibility tree, which
   * is right for a label nobody should lose but wrong here — this text is
   * not meant to be read by anyone, sighted or not, only found by the
   * search index, and `display: none` is both the correct semantics for
   * that and the cheaper one: a `display: none` subtree costs no layout or
   * paint at all, however long the text inside it, which is the entire
   * point of splitting the snippet in the first place.
   */
  .search .search-result__snippet-rest {
    display: none;
  }

  .search .search-result__chips,
  .search .search-result__extra {
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
