</style>

<style>
  /*
   * Code review finding (2026-09-05): this rule was originally inside the
   * `is:global` block above, as `:global(html.js) .search .search__toolbar`.
   * `:global()` is a *scoped-style* escape hatch — it has no meaning once the
   * whole block is already `is:global`, so Astro passed the text through
   * unchanged and browsers rejected the unknown `:global` pseudo-class
   * outright, dropping the whole rule. The toolbar's `display: none` above
   * then had nothing to override it, so the search box and type pills never
   * rendered, with or without JavaScript, in either locale.
   *
   * The fix is this second, ordinary **scoped** `<style>` block, holding only
   * the two rules that need `:global()` to mean something:
   * `[problemsSegment].astro` proves the same pattern compiles correctly
   * (`:global(html.js) .problems__toolbar` → `html.js
   * .problems__toolbar[data-astro-cid-…]`) — verified for this page too, via
   * a real Chromium instance's `document.styleSheets` and `getBoundingClientRect`
   * showing the toolbar with a real, non-zero size once `html.js` is present.
   *
   * The big `is:global` block above stays global on purpose (same SCF-06
   * page-weight reason `[glossarySegment].astro` documents): this page lists
   * up to ~280 result cards, and a `data-astro-cid-…` attribute on every
   * child of every card is real, measured page weight for no reader benefit.
   * Only this one small, single-element rule needs real scoping so
   * `:global()` inside it means what it says.
   */
  .search__toolbar {
    display: none;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    align-items: center;
  }

  :global(html.js) .search__toolbar {
    display: flex;
  }
</style>
